1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,055
In 2019, nearly 30 million people took a cruise.

2
00:00:04,055 --> 00:00:08,410
And by 2020, that number had collapsed to under 6 million.

3
00:00:08,410 --> 00:00:20,215
While Carnival, the largest cruise company on the planet, lost more than $10 billion in a single year and watched its debt explode from roughly $10 billion to almost $35 billion.

4
00:00:20,215 --> 00:00:27,760
If you had wanted to bet against an entire industry in the spring of 2020, cruising would have been the easiest bet in the world to make.

5
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:30,085
So here's the part that doesn't make sense.

6
00:00:30,085 --> 00:00:39,015
As of the middle of 2026, cruise lines around the world have placed orders for 80 new ships with a total construction bill of roughly $90 billion.

7
00:00:39,015 --> 00:00:47,535
And that number has climbed almost every month for over a year, from about 70 billion at the end of 2025 to over 90 billion today.

8
00:00:47,535 --> 00:00:54,190
So the industry that looked finished four years ago is now placing the biggest bet on its own future it has ever placed.

9
00:00:54,190 --> 00:01:10,780
In 2021, across the entire global cruise industry, exactly one new ship order was placed for the whole year, compared with 2017 and 2018, the two busiest ordering years in the industry's history, when companies were racing each other just to reserve a shipyard slot.

10
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:16,245
The order count went up to eight ships in 2022 and 11 in 2023.

11
00:01:16,245 --> 00:01:19,925
That was just a small sign of life rather than a real recovery.

12
00:01:19,925 --> 00:01:22,910
And then without much warning, everything changed.

13
00:01:22,910 --> 00:01:28,135
People didn't just come back to cruising, they came back in numbers nobody had predicted.

14
00:01:28,135 --> 00:01:36,050
In 2025, a record 37.2 million people took a cruise, up from 34.6 million the year before.

15
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:42,465
And the industry's trade group, CLIA, now expects that number to reach 42 million by 2028.

16
00:01:42,465 --> 00:01:49,180
Almost 90% of past cruisers say they want to go again, the highest repeat interest figure ever recorded.

17
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:53,455
Nearly a third of last year's passengers had never cruised before at all.

18
00:01:53,455 --> 00:01:58,525
It turns out a cruise ship has one advantage a hotel doesn't, which is that it can move.

19
00:01:58,525 --> 00:02:01,845
And it can go anywhere on the planet the moment demand shifts.

20
00:02:01,845 --> 00:02:07,175
Once travel reopened, that flexibility turned into the industry's greatest strength.

21
00:02:07,175 --> 00:02:13,535
So the cruise lines, watching the demand go high, made a collective decision to build and build more than ever before.

22
00:02:13,535 --> 00:02:20,510
While every cruise company is ordering new ships, the four biggest companies in the industry are not playing the same game though.

23
00:02:20,510 --> 00:02:23,450
And the differences say a lot about each of them.

24
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:38,630
Norwegian Cruise Line currently holds the largest order count of any company, 17 new ships, adding roughly 47,000 berths across its three brands: mainstream Norwegian, midrange Oceania, and ultra luxury Regent Seven Seas.

25
00:02:38,630 --> 00:02:42,075
MSC Cruises is playing a different game entirely.

26
00:02:42,075 --> 00:02:53,190
In December 2025, it signed a deal with the German shipyard Meyer Werft for four ships plus an option for two more worth about $11.7 billion US.

27
00:02:53,190 --> 00:03:07,390
What makes the deal remarkable is that Meyer Werft, a 230-year-old shipyard, had come close enough to bankruptcy a year earlier that the German government had to take an 80% ownership stake just to keep it alive.

28
00:03:07,390 --> 00:03:14,770
So, MSC's order didn't only buy six ships, it became just the thing keeping an entire historic shipyard alive.

